IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `wk-nouns`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for Business Activities Affecting Workers (2025), section 4
-- (non-compete clauses) and section 5 (other restrictive, exclusionary, or predatory
-- employment conditions).
--
-- AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW (n.1 [pdf page 2]). These are terms between an
-- employer and its workers: no agreement between businesses and no finding that
-- businesses compete for workers is read here. Each result is a sentence of the text with
-- its own modal ("can violate", "may investigate", "can be anticompetitive"). The
-- findings are the caller's; FALSE means a finding has not been made, and the text
-- attaches no statement to the contrary finding. EMPTY means no sentence of the section
-- applies; it is not a finding that the term is lawful.
--
-- Not encoded from section 4 (NOTES.md, coverage): the 2020 deferred prosecution
-- agreement and the FTC's enforcement actions and merger orders (past actions, not
-- rules); the FTC's April 2024 rule, its setting aside and its appeal, which the text
-- dates and sends the reader to ftc.gov for (n.30-n.32).

§ `Sections 4 and 5: employment terms that restrict workers`

§§ `Section 4: non-compete clauses`

DECLARE `What section 4 says of the term` IS ONE OF
    `non-compete clauses that restrict workers from switching jobs or starting a competing business can violate the antitrust laws`
    `the agencies may investigate and take action against non-competes and other restraints on worker mobility that limit competition`
    `non-competes may also violate other federal laws, such as the National Labor Relations Act and the Packers and Stockyards Act`
    `non-competes can also violate state laws, including laws banning unfair methods of competition and unfair or deceptive practices, as well as statutes banning or restricting some or all non-competes`

-- 4 [pdf pages 8-9], for a non-compete clause, in the text's order:
--   * "Non-compete clauses that restrict workers from switching jobs or starting a
--     competing business can violate the antitrust laws." (n.26) The relative clause has no
--     comma, so it is read as a condition, needing either finding (fork F26, which keeps
--     the descriptive reading as its alternative).
--   * "The Agencies may investigate and take action against non-competes and other
--     restraints on worker mobility that limit competition." "That limit competition" is
--     read as restricting which non-competes, so it needs the finding (fork F14).
--   * "Non-competes may also violate other federal laws, such as the National Labor
--     Relations Act and the Packers and Stockyards Act" (n.33, n.34), and "can also
--     violate state laws" (n.35): said of non-competes without condition.
-- Another kind of term: EMPTY (section 5 speaks of those; fork F18).
@ref 4 [pdf pages 8-9]
GIVEN t IS A `Employment term`
GIVETH A LIST OF `What section 4 says of the term`
`4: what section 4 says of the term` MEANS
    IF t's `the kind of term` EQUALS `a non-compete clause`
    THEN catMaybes (LIST (IF t's `it restricts the worker from switching jobs` OR t's `it restricts the worker from starting a competing business` THEN JUST `non-compete clauses that restrict workers from switching jobs or starting a competing business can violate the antitrust laws` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF t's `it limits competition` THEN JUST `the agencies may investigate and take action against non-competes and other restraints on worker mobility that limit competition` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (JUST `non-competes may also violate other federal laws, such as the National Labor Relations Act and the Packers and Stockyards Act`),
                         (JUST `non-competes can also violate state laws, including laws banning unfair methods of competition and unfair or deceptive practices, as well as statutes banning or restricting some or all non-competes`))
    ELSE EMPTY

§§ `Section 5: other restrictive, exclusionary, or predatory employment conditions`

DECLARE `What section 5 says of the term` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies may also investigate and take action against other restrictive agreements that impede worker mobility or otherwise undermine competition`
    `non-disclosure agreements can violate the antitrust laws when they span such a large scope of information that they function to prevent workers from seeking or accepting other work or starting a business after they leave their job`
    `a non-disclosure agreement drafted so broadly as to prohibit disclosure of any information that is usable in or relates to an industry may be unlawful`
    `non-disclosure agreements can also violate federal law when they are worded so broadly as to suggest that workers who report potential violations of law, or who cooperate with a government investigation, could face lawsuits and adverse employment consequences`
    `depending on the facts and circumstances, a training repayment agreement provision can be anticompetitive`
    `depending on the facts and circumstances, a non-solicitation agreement that prohibits a worker from soliciting former clients or customers of the employer can be anticompetitive`
    `depending on the facts and circumstances, an exit fee or liquidated damages provision can be anticompetitive`
    `the provision is the text's own example of when it can be anticompetitive: it functions to prevent the worker from working for another firm, seeking or accepting another job, or starting a business`
    `such provisions raise many of the same antitrust concerns as non-competes`
    `the agencies may investigate if there are indications that such a restrictive condition on workers is harming competition`
    `state law also sets limits on training repayment requirements and NDA restrictions`

-- 5 [pdf pages 10-11], for any kind of term but a non-compete clause, in the text's order:
--   * "The Agencies may also investigate and take action against other restrictive
--     agreements that impede worker mobility or otherwise undermine competition": needs
--     the finding (fork F14); for every kind, the unnamed one included.
--   * Non-disclosure agreements "can violate the antitrust laws when they span such a
--     large scope of information that they function to prevent workers from seeking or
--     accepting other work or starting a business": "when" is a condition, so this needs
--     the finding (fork F16). One "drafted so broadly as to prohibit disclosure of any
--     information that is 'usable in' or 'relates to' an industry may be unlawful" (n.36).
--     They "can also violate federal law when they are worded so broadly as to suggest"
--     that workers who report or cooperate "could face lawsuits and adverse employment
--     consequences" (n.37).
--   * Training repayment, non-solicitation, and exit fee or liquidated damages provisions:
--     "Depending on the facts and circumstances" each "can be anticompetitive", with no
--     condition; "such as if" each functions to prevent the worker from working for
--     another firm or starting a business is the text's example, not a condition, so the
--     example sentence is given only when that finding is made, beside the other (fork
--     F16).
--   * "Such provisions raise many of the same antitrust concerns as non-competes, and the
--     Agencies may investigate if there are indications that such a restrictive condition
--     on workers is harming competition." [pdf page 11]. The first clause is read for the
--     kinds the bullets name ("These types of restrictions"), a non-disclosure agreement
--     only when it is broad in one of the bullet's two antitrust ways (it prevents leaving,
--     or it reaches any information usable in or relating to an industry), since the
--     bullet's "when they span such a large scope" and Overview item 5's "overly broad
--     non-disclosure agreements" [pdf page 3] say which are meant (fork F27). The second
--     clause's "such a restrictive condition" is read for every kind, the unnamed one
--     included, since the bullets are "examples" that "illustrate how restrictive
--     conditions could potentially violate the antitrust laws" (fork F17); it needs the
--     finding.
--   * "State law also sets limits on training repayment requirements and NDA
--     restrictions." (n.43): for those two kinds, without condition (fork F28).
-- A non-compete clause: EMPTY (fork F18).
@ref 5 [pdf pages 10-11]
GIVEN t IS A `Employment term`
GIVETH A LIST OF `What section 5 says of the term`
`5: what section 5 says of the term` MEANS
    IF kind EQUALS `a non-compete clause`
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE catMaybes (LIST (IF t's `it impedes worker mobility or otherwise undermines competition` THEN JUST `the agencies may also investigate and take action against other restrictive agreements that impede worker mobility or otherwise undermine competition` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF nda AND prevents THEN JUST `non-disclosure agreements can violate the antitrust laws when they span such a large scope of information that they function to prevent workers from seeking or accepting other work or starting a business after they leave their job` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF nda AND t's `it prohibits disclosure of any information that is usable in or relates to an industry` THEN JUST `a non-disclosure agreement drafted so broadly as to prohibit disclosure of any information that is usable in or relates to an industry may be unlawful` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF nda AND t's `it is worded so broadly as to suggest that workers who report potential violations of law, or who cooperate with a government investigation, could face lawsuits and adverse employment consequences` THEN JUST `non-disclosure agreements can also violate federal law when they are worded so broadly as to suggest that workers who report potential violations of law, or who cooperate with a government investigation, could face lawsuits and adverse employment consequences` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF kind EQUALS `a training repayment agreement provision` THEN JUST `depending on the facts and circumstances, a training repayment agreement provision can be anticompetitive` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF kind EQUALS `a non-solicitation agreement that prohibits the worker from soliciting former clients or customers of the employer` THEN JUST `depending on the facts and circumstances, a non-solicitation agreement that prohibits a worker from soliciting former clients or customers of the employer can be anticompetitive` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF kind EQUALS `an exit fee or liquidated damages provision` THEN JUST `depending on the facts and circumstances, an exit fee or liquidated damages provision can be anticompetitive` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF hedged AND prevents THEN JUST `the provision is the text's own example of when it can be anticompetitive: it functions to prevent the worker from working for another firm, seeking or accepting another job, or starting a business` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF hedged OR (nda AND (prevents OR t's `it prohibits disclosure of any information that is usable in or relates to an industry`)) THEN JUST `such provisions raise many of the same antitrust concerns as non-competes` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF t's `there are indications that it is harming competition` THEN JUST `the agencies may investigate if there are indications that such a restrictive condition on workers is harming competition` ELSE NOTHING),
                         (IF nda OR kind EQUALS `a training repayment agreement provision` THEN JUST `state law also sets limits on training repayment requirements and NDA restrictions` ELSE NOTHING))
  WHERE
    kind MEANS t's `the kind of term`
    nda MEANS kind EQUALS `a non-disclosure agreement`
    prevents MEANS t's `it functions to prevent the worker from seeking, accepting or working in another job, or from starting a business`
    hedged MEANS    kind EQUALS `a training repayment agreement provision`
                 OR kind EQUALS `a non-solicitation agreement that prohibits the worker from soliciting former clients or customers of the employer`
                 OR kind EQUALS `an exit fee or liquidated damages provision`
